@attribute [Route("/components/" + nameof(HxGoogleTagManager))]
@attribute [Route("/components/" + nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker))]
@using Havit.Blazor.GoogleTagManager

<ApiDoc Type="typeof(HxGoogleTagManager)">
    <DocAlert Type="DocAlertType.Warning">
        <code>HxGoogleTagManager</code> and related components are distributed in a separate NuGet package: <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Havit.Blazor.GoogleTagManager">Havit.Blazor.GoogleTagManager</a>.
    </DocAlert>

	<DocHeading Title="Basic usage" />
	<p>Install GTM support through application startup:</p>
	<CodeSnippet File="~\Pages\Components\HxGoogleTagManagerDoc\HxGoogleTagManager_ServiceRegistration.CodeSnippet.cs" />
</ApiDoc>

<ApiDoc Type="typeof(HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker)">
    <DocHeading Title="Basic usage" Id="HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker-basic-usage" />
	<p>Install GTM support through application startup (see above) and add <code>HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker</code> to <code>App.razor</code> or <code>MainLayout.razor</code></p>
	<CodeSnippet Language="html">@("<HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker />")</CodeSnippet>

	<DocHeading Title="Render modes" Id="HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker-render-modes" Level="3" />
	<p>
		The component supports every render mode. What it does depends on where it ends up being rendered:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Static SSR and prerendering</strong> - the GTM snippet is emitted inline as a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag
			into the HTML response, so GTM starts loading while the page is still being parsed. No JS interop is available
			in this phase, which is why the tracker cannot rely on <code>IHxGoogleTagManager</code> here.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Static SSR with <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/fundamentals/navigation#enhanced-navigation-and-form-handling">enhanced navigation</a></strong> -
			an inline <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> is not executed again when Blazor patches a new page into the DOM,
			so page-views for enhanced navigations are pushed by a
			<a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/fundamentals/startup#javascript-initializers">JS initializer</a>
			shipped with the package, which listens for the <code>enhancedload</code> event. Nothing needs to be referenced
			or configured - Blazor loads JS initializers automatically.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Interactive Server, WebAssembly and Auto</strong> - page-views are pushed in reaction to
			<code>NavigationManager.LocationChanged</code>, which is what covers navigations handled by the interactive router.
			The <code>enhancedload</code> event is not raised for those.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<DocHeading Title="Where to place the component" Id="HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker-placement" Level="3" />
	<p>
		In a Blazor Web App the render mode the component ends up with is the render mode of the position it is rendered in,
		and that decides which of the paths above it can take:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<code>App.razor</code> is always statically rendered, so the component emits the inline snippet on every response.
			This gives the earliest possible GTM load, and enhanced navigations are covered by the JS initializer -
			but navigations performed by an interactive router are not seen.
		</li>
		<li>
			A layout or page inside an interactive island is rendered interactively, so the component subscribes to
			<code>LocationChanged</code> and covers interactive routing. With
			<a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/components/render-modes#static-ssr-pages-in-an-interactive-app">global interactivity</a>
			this is the placement that covers the whole app.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Rendering the component more than once (for example in <code>App.razor</code> <em>and</em> in the layout) is safe.
		GTM initialization and automatic page-views are deduplicated in JavaScript through shared
		<code>window.hxGoogleTagManager</code> state, so a navigation seen by both paths still produces a single event.
		An explicit <code>IHxGoogleTagManager.PushPageViewAsync()</code> call is never deduplicated - it always pushes.
	</p>

	<DocHeading Title="Events pushed to the data-layer" Id="HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker-events" Level="3" />
	<ul>
		<li>
			<code>gtm.js</code> and <code>pageview</code> - once per document load, from the GTM snippet itself.
			(<code>pageview</code> is not part of Google's snippet; it is pushed for backwards compatibility.)
		</li>
		<li>
			<code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.PageViewEventName)</code> (<code>virtualPageView</code> by default) - once per navigation,
			including enhanced navigations and interactive routing. Configure a trigger for it in your GTM container;
			the <code>pageview</code> event alone fires only on real page loads.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		By default the page the document was loaded with is announced by both - once as <code>pageview</code> and once as
		<code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.PageViewEventName)</code>. Which of the two conventions your container follows
		decides whether that is what you want:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.PageViewEventName)</code> is the single source of truth and <code>pageview</code>
			is ignored - keep <code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.EnableInitialPageViewTracking)</code> at its default <code>true</code>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<code>pageview</code> (or the container's built-in Page View trigger) covers real document loads and
			<code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.PageViewEventName)</code> only the navigations that follow - set
			<code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.EnableInitialPageViewTracking)</code> to <code>false</code>,
			otherwise the landing page of every session is counted twice.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		The option affects automatic tracking only. An explicit <code>IHxGoogleTagManager.PushPageViewAsync()</code> call always pushes.
	</p>

	<DocHeading Title="Content Security Policy" Id="HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker-csp" Level="3" />
	<p>
		When a Content Security Policy with <code>nonce-*</code> is in use, pass the nonce to the inline snippet:
	</p>
	<CodeSnippet Language="html">@("<HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker Nonce=\"@Nonce\" />")</CodeSnippet>
</ApiDoc>
